<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Talysh Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1826 CE · capital Lankaran</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Caspian khanate in the subtropical lowlands and forests of Talysh, divided between Russia and Persia in 1828.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1747, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-khan-of-talysh">Jamal al-Din Khan</a>, called Qara Khan, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talysh">Talysh</a> district an independent khanate, with its capital first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astara">Astara</a> and then Lankaran on the Caspian shore. His family claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>. The district had earlier been governed for the shahs by local Talysh chiefs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans ruled Talysh-speaking and Azerbaijani villages in fertile lowlands and forested mountains, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and fish for Caspian trade. Their small state survived by balancing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quba-khanate">Quba Khanate</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-mustafa-khan">Mir Mustafa Khan</a>, who ruled from 1786, sought Russian protection against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Russian forces stormed Lankaran on 1 January 1813, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> later that year confirmed Russian protection. After Persian reoccupation in 1826, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> in 1828 split <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talysh">Talysh</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astara">Astara</a> river, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed the northern part.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/talysh-khanate.jpg" alt="Talysh Khanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ismayil Ahmadli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Talysh_Khanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1826 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lankaran</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jamal al-Din Khan, Mir Mustafa Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Talysh; Azerbaijani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Talysh, Lankaran Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=talysh-khanate" aria-label="Talysh Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5892 787 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Talysh Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6212 1043L6298 1144M6212 1043L6298 1144M6212 1043L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6298 1144h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6212 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6212 1043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lankaran. <a href="/atlas?year=1786&amp;focus=talysh-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
